===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|j}} and {{liushu|i|nocap=1}}: {{och-l|束}} or {{och-l|橐}} borrowed for sound {{och-l|東}}, with a horizontal stroke added as a distinguishing mark to differentiate the character from 束.

Alternatively, it is also thought to be a {{liushu|p|nocap=yes}} of either a bundle of [[faggot|faggots]] tied together or a bag tied at both ends (like a cellophane-wrapped candy with the ends twisted).

Interpreted in the {{w|Shuowen Jiezi}} as an {{liushu|ic|nocap=yes}} the sun ({{zh-l|*日}}) rising behind a tree ({{zh-l|*木}}). According to {{zh-ref|Allan (1991)}} p. 47-50, the character represents the {{w|Fu Sang}} tree. However, the original form is rather different, hence any interpretation tied to a tree is most likely a folk etymology.

See the top component of [[曹]] in the original version. See also [[柬]], [[熏]], [[西]] and [[鹵]]/[[卤]].

===Etymology===
Cognate with {{cog|cdm|थोङ्‌सा|tr=thoŋ‑|t=[[lighten]]; [[be]] [[bright]]}} and {{cog|cdm|थोङ्‌ह|tr=thoŋh|t=[[awake]] [[time]] (as opposed to [[dream]] time)}} ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}). Perhaps related to or influenced by {{och-l|暾|gloss=[[rising]] [[sun]]}}.

